
Representable object bounds (option iii, 2026-10-17). Three further changes, each necessary and
none sufficient alone: seven size-class strides raised by 1-4 units so every large class's stride
is a multiple of its own bounds granule; a granule-sized group header so the first slot's base is a
multiple of it too; and enframe stepping each object's base DOWN to a granule-aligned offset within
its slot. Without them the compressed encoder truncates an unaligned base DOWNWARD and the object
gains authority below itself -- measured against the RTL bounds model over 6060 (class, request,
slot, offset-cycle) cases, 1497 decode wrong with the step-down alone and a 16-byte header, the
first being class 36 at n=16480 slot 0 offset 0, whose base 0x400000010 decodes as
[0x400000000, 0x400004080), and 0 with all three in place.

The header costs nothing: alloc_group already rounds the group UP to a page, and over every class
and every reachable cnt that rounding leaves at least the pad. The margin is exact in places (class
36 at cnt 7 has 16 bytes for a 16-byte pad), so cap_hdr_fits asserts it and a future table or count
change fails loudly instead of overrunning the group. The step-down is down and never up, so it
cannot exceed slack, and it is a no-op for any granule at or below UNIT -- every length below
8 KiB -- so the double-free offset cycling is untouched there.

size_to_class and the slot/class selection are untouched: the request is rounded in the runtime's
own malloc wrapper, outside musl, so set_size records exactly what the capability covers.
check-mallocng-policy.py pins the seven entries and their values and refuses any other difference.

These live here rather than in a sibling patch because build-sdk.sh checks currency by requiring
every patch to reverse-apply INDEPENDENTLY, and a patch editing the lines this one adds makes this
one un-reversible -- the SDK build then refuses with "musl source is not current".

